/* RmlUi overlay for the active-buff icon strip (CBuffStrip, BuffStrip.h/.cpp -- renamed from the
   legacy CNewUIBuffWindow at port time, see .ai-os/memory/tasks/rmlui-ui-architecture-amendment.md's
   "Tracked deferral" section for the naming policy). Second pilot of the CNewUIObj-tier RmlUi
   adapter pattern (2026-08-31) -- see docs/rmlui-ui-system/newui-tier-adapter.md and this file's
   sibling BuffStrip.h for the full rationale of the two remaining deliberate simplifications from
   the original (no right-click-cancel, plain-text tooltip).

   Icon atlas: named @spritesheet rects, one per 20x28 tile, 10 columns x 9 rows (0-89) generated
   per atlas below -- the SAME mechanism every other migrated window's icons already use (see e.g.
   char_make.rcss's charmake-job-sheet). An earlier attempt used a clipped-oversized-image
   technique instead (a small overflow:hidden container holding a full-atlas-sized image,
   positioned by a per-item negative offset) specifically to avoid hand-enumerating ~160 rects --
   confirmed by direct in-game testing NOT to work in this RmlUi build: ContainerBox::Close()
   submits a box's scrollable-overflow rect (which ElementUtilities::GetClippingRegion()'s
   has_clipping_content check reads to decide whether overflow:hidden/clip:always actually produce
   a scissor) before ClosePositionedElements() ever places an absolutely-positioned child, so the
   container always computes "nothing to clip" for exactly this shape of content, regardless of
   its own overflow/clip properties. Rather than keep chasing that, the rects are generated
   (BuildIconDecorator(), BuffStrip.cpp) instead of hand-authored -- see BuffEntry::decorator's
   comment in BuffStrip.h for the full story, including why generation is capped at 90 tiles/atlas
   (rows 0-8) rather than covering eBuffState's full numeric range. */

